A stack view decoded with nothing to arrange arranges nothing.

-[NSStackView initWithCoder:] created an EMPTY _arrangedSubviews and decoded only orientation and
spacing. Every nib loaded stack view therefore had nothing to arrange, -layout walked an empty
list, and the children kept the origin Interface Builder wrote. For a vertical stack that is every
child at the same place.

NOTHING ELSE PLACES THEM EITHER, which is what makes this total rather than cosmetic. The
constraint solver deliberately skips arranged subviews, because the stack is supposed to own them,
so an empty arranged list means no owner at all.

THE ARCHIVE SAYS WHAT THEY ARE. There is no NSStackViewViews key. The flag
NSStackViewHasFlatViewHierarchy says the arranged views ARE the decoded subviews rather than living
inside gravity containers, and it is true on all six stacks in iA Writer GeneralPreferences.nib.
The non flat form, where NSStackViewBeginningContainer holds them, is NOT handled and will still
arrange nothing.

MEASURED. iA Writer Preferences had two 38 point rows at the same local origin, one exactly on top
of the other:

  before   NSStackView 182x38 at 207,395
             NSStackView 182x38 at 0,0
             NSStackView 161x38 at 0,0
  after    NSStackView 172x38 at 207,395
             NSStackView 172x12 at 0,26
             NSStackView 172x12 at 0,0

Overlapping sibling pairs in that pane go from 1 to 0, and the two labels that follow those rows by
firstBaseline now sit on separate lines: Appearance and Dock icon are legible where they were drawn
through each other.

WHAT IS STILL WRONG, and it is visible. The rows are now SQUASHED. The stack is 38 points tall and
its two rows want about 91, so the checkboxes and the Light and Dark radios inside them are clipped
to 12 points. That is the next link and it is a solver ordering problem, not a stack view one: the
stack reports its arranged size as its intrinsic size already, but the solver derives its height
from BOTH ends, pinning the top at 433 and solving the bottom from the NSBox beneath it, when the
box should instead follow the stack intrinsic height. An intrinsic size has to win over a derived
one when the other end is itself underdetermined.

So this trades an unreadable overlap for a legible but compressed pair of rows. It is landed
because a stack view that arranges nothing is never correct, and because the roster is unaffected.

Gates: dts 69 of 69, patch record PASS, roster sweep 7 of 7 CONTENT and roster input 7 of 7, with
iA Writer looked at.

--- a/AppKit/NSStackView.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSStackView.m
@@ -78,6 +78,28 @@
         _orientation = [coder decodeIntegerForKey: @"NSStackViewOrientation"];
         if ([coder containsValueForKey: @"NSStackViewSpacing"])
             _spacing = [coder decodeDoubleForKey: @"NSStackViewSpacing"];
+
+        /*
+         * A STACK VIEW DECODED WITH NOTHING TO ARRANGE ARRANGES NOTHING.
+         *
+         * This left _arrangedSubviews empty, so -layout walked an empty list and every nib loaded
+         * stack view placed none of its children. They keep the origin Interface Builder wrote,
+         * which for a vertical stack is every child at the same place: iA Writer Preferences has
+         * two 38 point rows both at local 0,0, one exactly on top of the other, and the labels
+         * that follow those rows by firstBaseline both land on y=417.
+         *
+         * Nothing else places them either. The constraint solver deliberately skips arranged
+         * subviews, because the stack is supposed to own them, so an empty arranged list means no
+         * owner at all.
+         *
+         * THE ARCHIVE SAYS WHAT THEY ARE. There is no NSStackViewViews key; the flag
+         * NSStackViewHasFlatViewHierarchy says the arranged views ARE the decoded subviews rather
+         * than living inside gravity containers, and it is true on all six stacks in that nib. The
+         * non flat form, where NSStackViewBeginningContainer holds them, is NOT handled here and
+         * will still arrange nothing.
+         */
+        if ([coder decodeBoolForKey: @"NSStackViewHasFlatViewHierarchy"])
+            [_arrangedSubviews addObjectsFromArray: [self subviews]];
     }
     return self;
 }
